nly one** of the two sunny lines (say \\(L_{t}^{(1)}\\)) and compensate the missing point by adding the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) back into the family.  This increases the number of sunny lines by one and restores the total to \\(n\\).  Hence every odd \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) is also attainable.\n\n   \u2013  Summarising, for each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) we have exhibited an explicit set of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(P\\).\n\n4. **The exceptional case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   For \\(n=3\\) the set \\(P\\) consists of the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n   They can be covered by the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=x,\\qquad y=x+1,\\qquad y=-2x+5,\n   \\]\n\n   which are pairwise distinct and none is parallel to the forbidden directions.  Hence \\(k=3\\) is also possible when \\(n=3\\).\n\n   Combining this with the construction above for \\(k\\le n-1\\) gives the complete list of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nP=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |P|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **non\u2011sunny** if it is parallel to one of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{h}:\\;y=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_{v}:\\;x=\\text{const},\\qquad \n\\ell_{d}:\\;x+y=\\text{const},\n\\]\n\nand **sunny** otherwise.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  At least one non\u2011sunny line is necessary for \\(n\\ge 4\\)\n\nAssume, to the contrary, that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) sunny lines covers \\(P\\).  \nEvery sunny line has a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently any two distinct sunny\nlines intersect in at most one point of \\(P\\).  \n\nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nA=(1,n),\\qquad B=(n,1),\\qquad C=\\Bigl(\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\n          \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\Bigr).\n\\]\n\nThey are pairwise non\u2011collinear (the line through \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) has slope \\(-1\\), which is forbidden; the\nother two lines have slopes different from \\(-1\\) and from each other).  \nHence three distinct sunny lines are required to cover \\(A,B,C\\).\n\nAll remaining points of \\(P\\) lie on one of the three lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nIf none of these three lines belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\), each of the remaining \\(|P|-3\\) points must be\ncovered by a sunny line that is different from the three already used for \\(A,B,C\\).  Thus at least\n\\(3+ (|P|-3)=|P|\\) distinct lines would be needed, contradicting \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\) because\n\\(|P|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2>n\\) for \\(n\\ge 4\\).\n\nTherefore for \\(n\\ge 4\\) any covering family must contain at least one non\u2011sunny line; i.e.\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1\\qquad (n\\ge 4). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A universal covering with only non\u2011sunny lines\n\nThe \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1,\\qquad x+y=n+1 \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nare all non\u2011sunny and together cover every point of \\(P\\).  Indeed, a point \\((a,b)\\in P\\) satisfies\n\\(a\\le n-1\\) or \\(a=n\\).  If \\(a\\le n-1\\) it lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\); if \\(a=n\\) then\n\\(b=1\\) and \\((n,1)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\).  Hence (2) is a valid covering with\n\\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Replacing vertical lines by sunny lines\n\nFor an integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-1\\) the set of points on the vertical line \\(x=t\\) is  \n\n\\[\nV_{t}=\\{(t,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-t\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine two sunny lines of slope \\(1\\)\n\n\\[\nL_{t}^{(1)}:\\;y=x-t+1,\\qquad\nL_{t}^{(2)}:\\;y=x-t+2 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBoth lines are sunny because their slope \\(1\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\).  \nA direct substitution shows that\n\n\\[\nV_{t}= \\bigl(L_{t}^{(1)}\\cup L_{t}^{(2)}\\bigr)\\cap P . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus the pair \\(\\{L_{t}^{(1)},L_{t}^{(2)}\\}\\) covers exactly the same points of \\(P\\) as the\nvertical line \\(x=t\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Obtaining any prescribed number of sunny lines\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n*If \\(k\\) is even*, write \\(k=2m\\) with \\(0\\le m\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\).\nReplace the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=m\\) in (2) by the \\(2m\\) sunny lines\n\\(\\{L_{t}^{(1)},L_{t}^{(2)}\\}_{t=1}^{m}\\).  \nThe diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) is retained.  \nThe resulting family consists of  \n\n\\[\n(n-1-m)\\text{ vertical lines }+1\\text{ diagonal }+2m\\text{ sunny lines}=n+m\n\\]\n\nlines.  To bring the total back to \\(n\\) we simply **discard** the diagonal line (which is\nnon\u2011sunny).  The final family therefore contains exactly \\(2m=k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny\nlines, and by (4) it still covers all points of \\(P\\).\n\n*If \\(k\\) is odd*, write \\(k=2m+1\\) with \\(0\\le m\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\rfloor\\).\nProceed as in the even case with the same replacement of the first \\(m\\) vertical lines.\nNow keep the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (one non\u2011sunny line) and, for the next vertical line\n\\(x=m+1\\), keep **only** the sunny line \\(L_{m+1}^{(1)}\\) (one sunny line) and discard its partner\n\\(L_{m+1}^{(2)}\\).  The total number of lines is\n\n\\[\n(n-1-m-1) \\text{ remaining verticals }+1\\text{ diagonal }+2m\\text{ sunny }+1\\text{ extra sunny }\n= n .\n\\]\n\nThe family now contains \\(2m+1=k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, and by construction it\ncovers every point of \\(P\\).\n\nThus for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) we have exhibited a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines\ncontaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(P\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nWhen \\(n=3\\) the set \\(P\\) consists of the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\ny=x,\\qquad y=x+1,\\qquad y=-2x+5\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct, none is parallel to the forbidden directions, and each point of \\(P\\) lies on\none of them.  Hence \\(k=3\\) is attainable for \\(n=3\\).\n\nTogether with the construction of Section\u202f4 (which yields the values \\(k=0,1,2\\) for \\(n=3\\) as\nwell) we obtain the complete list of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n-1\\,\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nand, in the special case \\(n=3\\), the value \\(k=n\\) is also possible.  No other values of \\(k\\) can\noccur, because for \\(n\\ge4\\) at least one non\u2011sunny line is forced (inequality (1)), and for each\n\\(k\\) in the stated range we have provided an explicit construction.  \u220e"
