2n-(2n-1)\\)).  \n  The vertical lines cover every point whose first coordinate is\n  \\(\\le n-1\\); the only point of \\(S_n\\) not covered by them is \\((n,1)\\),\n  which lies on \\(L\\).  Hence all points of \\(S_n\\) are covered and exactly\n  one line is sunny.  Thus \\(k=1\\) is also attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume that a family of \\(n\\) lines satisfying the conditions exists and that\nexactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  Let  \n\n\\[\nV\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad\nH\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad\nD\\subseteq\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the sets of indices of the vertical, horizontal and diagonal lines that\nappear among the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  Then  \n\n\\[\n|V|+|H|+|D|=n-k. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) is **uncovered by the non‑sunny lines** iff  \n\n\\[\na\\notin V,\\qquad b\\notin H,\\qquad a+b\\notin D. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nDenote  \n\n\\[\nA:=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus V,\\qquad\nB:=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus H,\\qquad\nC:=\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus D .\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) we have  \n\n\\[\n|A|=n-|V|\\ge k,\\qquad |B|=n-|H|\\ge k,\\qquad |C|=(n)-|D|\\ge k. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1  \nIf \\(k\\ge 2\\) then there exists a point of \\(S_n\\) satisfying (2); i.e. at\nleast one point is not covered by any non‑sunny line.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(|A|\\ge 2\\) and \\(|B|\\ge 2\\), the set of sums  \n\n\\[\nS:=\\{a+b\\mid a\\in A,\\;b\\in B\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains at least three distinct integers (the smallest possible case is\n\\(A=\\{p,p+1\\}, B=\\{q,q+1\\}\\), giving the sums \\(p+q,\\;p+q+1,\\;p+q+2\\)).\nSince \\(|C|\\ge 2\\), at most two of those sums can belong to \\(C\\); consequently\nsome sum \\(s\\in S\\) lies **outside** \\(C\\), i.e. \\(s\\in D\\).  Choose\n\\(a\\in A,\\;b\\in B\\) with \\(a+b=s\\).  Then \\(a\\notin V,\\;b\\notin H\\) and\n\\(a+b\\notin D\\), so \\((a,b)\\) satisfies (2). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2  \nA sunny line can contain **at most one** point of the set\n\\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\in A,\\;b\\in B\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nTwo distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2)\\) with \\(a_1\\neq a_2\\) and\n\\(b_1\\neq b_2\\) determine a line whose slope is\n\\(\\displaystyle\\frac{b_2-b_1}{a_2-a_1}\\).  If the line were parallel to the\n\\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-coordinates would be equal; if it were parallel to the\n\\(y\\)-axis, the \\(x\\)-coordinates would be equal; if it were parallel to\n\\(x+y=0\\) we would have \\(b_2-b_1=-(a_2-a_1)\\), i.e. slope \\(-1\\).\nAll three possibilities are excluded for a sunny line.  Hence a sunny line\ncannot contain two points with different \\(a\\)‑coordinates **and** different\n\\(b\\)‑coordinates, which is exactly the situation for points taken from\n\\(A\\times B\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Consequence  \n\nAll points of \\(S_n\\) that are not covered by the non‑sunny lines belong to\n\\(A\\times B\\).  By Lemma&nbsp;2 each sunny line can cover at most one of them,\nso the number of sunny lines must be at least the number of such points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Impossibility of \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines\nexists.  By Lemma&nbsp;1 there is at least one point \\((a,b)\\) satisfying (2);\nhence \\((a,b)\\in A\\times B\\).  Lemma&nbsp;2 shows that a single sunny line can\ncover at most one point of \\(A\\times B\\).  Therefore we need **at least**\n\\(|A\\times B|\\) sunny lines.\n\nFrom (3) we have \\(|A|\\ge k\\) and \\(|B|\\ge k\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n|A\\times B|=|A|\\cdot|B|\\ge k^{2}\\ge 4 .\n\\]\n\nThus at least four points of \\(S_n\\) would have to be covered by sunny lines,\nbut we have only \\(k\\le n-2\\) sunny lines.  For \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have\n\\(k\\le n-2<4\\) when \\(k\\ge 2\\) (the only possibilities are \\(k=2,3,\\dots\\)),\na contradiction.  Hence no configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nThe constructions of Section&nbsp;3 show that \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are\nrealizable for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).  Lemma&nbsp;1–2 together with the counting\nargument of Section&nbsp;5 prove that no configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) can\nsatisfy the required conditions.  Therefore the set of admissible values of\n\\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Answer  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ the only possible values are }k=0\\text{\nor }k=1.}\n\\]"
