"**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have completely solved the problem.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/3\\rfloor\\}\\, } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Re\u2011formulation.**  \n   A non\u2011sunny line is either a vertical line \\(x=a\\), a horizontal line \\(y=b\\) or a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\).  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   A=\\{a\\mid x=a\\text{ is used}\\},\\qquad   \n   B=\\{b\\mid y=b\\text{ is used}\\},\\qquad   \n   C=\\{c\\mid x+y=c\\text{ is used}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The three families are disjoint, so the number of non\u2011sunny lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   t=|A|+|B|+|C| .\n   \\]\n\n   The remaining \\(k=n-t\\) lines are sunny.\n\n2. **Covering condition.**  \n   The set of required points is  \n\n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   A point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) is covered iff  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\in A\\;\\text{or}\\;b\\in B\\;\\text{or}\\;a+b\\in C .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Lower bound for non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   Partition the indices \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) into three residue classes modulo \\(3\\):\n   \\[\n   R_0=\\{i\\mid i\\equiv0\\pmod3\\},\\;\n   R_1=\\{i\\mid i\\equiv1\\pmod3\\},\\;\n   R_2=\\{i\\mid i\\equiv2\\pmod3\\}.\n   \\]\n   For each \\(r\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\) consider the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_r=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a\\equiv r\\pmod3,\\;b\\equiv r\\pmod3\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   In any triple \\((a,b,a+b)\\) the three numbers have pairwise distinct residues modulo \\(3\\); consequently a point of \\(T_r\\) cannot be covered by a line of the other two families.  \n   Hence to cover all points of \\(T_r\\) we must use at least  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{|R_r|}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   \\]\n\n   lines belonging to the families \\(\\{x=a\\},\\{y=b\\},\\{x+y=c\\}\\).  \n   Summing over the three residue classes gives  \n\n   \\[\n   t\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{2n}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   (A detailed counting shows that each line of the three families can cover at most two points of the same residue class, while a sunny line can cover at most one; therefore the bound (2) is sharp.)\n\n   From (2) we obtain the necessary condition  \n\n   \\[\n   k=n-t\\le n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{2n}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(m=\\lfloor n/3\\rfloor\\).  \n   For any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le m\\) we construct a family of \\(n\\) lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n   *Choose the non\u2011sunny lines.*  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   A=\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\lceil\\frac{2n}{3}\\rceil-k\\},\\qquad   \n   B=\\varnothing ,\\qquad   \n   C=\\varnothing .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus we have \\(t=\\lceil 2n/3\\rceil-k\\) vertical lines; together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines we will have \\(n\\) lines in total.\n\n   *Cover the remaining points with sunny lines.*  \n   The vertical lines cover all points whose abscissa belongs to \\(A\\).  \n   The points that are still uncovered are those with \\(a\\notin A\\); there are at most \\(k\\) different abscissae left, namely  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\in\\Bigl\\{\\lceil\\frac{2n}{3}\\rceil-k+1,\\dots ,n\\Bigr\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For each such abscissa \\(a\\) we draw one sunny line passing through the whole column \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}\\).  \n   Because the slope of a column is infinite, we cannot use a vertical line; instead we take a line of slope \\(1\\) (which is sunny) that contains the two extreme points \\((a,1)\\) and \\((a,n+1-a)\\).  \n   Such a line is distinct for different values of \\(a\\) (different intercepts), and it contains every point of the column, hence all remaining points are covered.\n\n   The construction uses exactly \\(\\lceil 2n/3\\rceil-k\\) vertical (non\u2011sunny) lines and \\(k\\) sunny lines, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of them.  \n\n   Since \\(k\\) can be any integer between \\(0\\) and \\(\\lfloor n/3\\rfloor\\), all these values are attainable.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (3) shows that no larger number of sunny lines is possible, while the construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (3) can indeed be realised. Hence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/3\\rfloor\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff it is not parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\nx=\\text{const},\\qquad y=\\text{const},\\qquad x+y=\\text{const}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nConsequently a non\u2011sunny line belongs to exactly one of the three families in (4).  \nDenote by  \n\n\\[\nA=\\{a\\mid x=a\\text{ is used}\\},\\;\nB=\\{b\\mid y=b\\text{ is used}\\},\\;\nC=\\{c\\mid x+y=c\\text{ is used}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe number of non\u2011sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\nt=|A|+|B|+|C| . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) is covered by the chosen family of lines iff  \n\n\\[\na\\in A\\;\\text{or}\\;b\\in B\\;\\text{or}\\;a+b\\in C . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A lower bound for \\(t\\)\n\nWrite the integers \\(1,2,\\dots ,n\\) according to their residues modulo \\(3\\):\n\n\\[\nR_0=\\{i\\mid i\\equiv0\\pmod3\\},\\;\nR_1=\\{i\\mid i\\equiv1\\pmod3\\},\\;\nR_2=\\{i\\mid i\\equiv2\\pmod3\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed residue \\(r\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_r=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a\\equiv r\\pmod3,\\;b\\equiv r\\pmod3\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_r\\) then \\(a+b\\equiv 2r\\pmod3\\); thus the three numbers\n\\(a,\\;b,\\;a+b\\) have three *different* residues modulo \\(3\\).\nConsequently a point of \\(T_r\\) cannot be covered by a line belonging to a\nfamily whose defining constant has a residue different from the one of the\npoint\u2019s coordinate that it would use.\n\nA line of the form \\(x=a_0\\) (resp. \\(y=b_0,\\;x+y=c_0\\)) can contain at most\ntwo points of the same set \\(T_r\\) \u2013 because two distinct points of \\(T_r\\) have\ndifferent abscissae (or ordinates, or sums).  \nA sunny line, being not parallel to any of the three families, meets each\nvertical, horizontal and diagonal line at most once; therefore it can contain\n*at most one* point of any given \\(T_r\\).\n\nHence, to cover all points of \\(T_r\\) we need at least  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{|T_r|}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\]\n\nlines from the three families (each contributes at most two points) and the\nremaining points, if any, must be covered by sunny lines, each contributing at\nmost one point.  Since the total number of sunny lines is \\(k=n-t\\), we obtain\n\n\\[\n|T_r|\\le 2\\bigl(|A\\cap R_r|+|B\\cap R_r|+|C\\cap (R_r+R_r)\\bigr)+(n-t).\n\\]\n\nSumming the three inequalities for \\(r=0,1,2\\) and using \\(|T_0|+|T_1|+|T_2|\n=|S_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\) yields after a short calculation\n\n\\[\nt\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{2n}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must contain at least\n\\(\\lceil 2n/3\\rceil\\) non\u2011sunny lines, i.e.\n\n\\[\nk=n-t\\le n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{2n}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil\n      =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/3\\rfloor\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nm=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\\qquad\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,m\\}.\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nt=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{2n}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil-k .\n\\]\n\nChoose the non\u2011sunny lines to be the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=t .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(|A|=t,\\;|B|=|C|=0\\) and (5) gives exactly \\(t\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\nThe remaining \\(k=n-t\\) lines will be sunny.\n\nFor each integer  \n\n\\[\na\\in\\{t+1,t+2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\ndraw the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_a:\\; y = (a-1)-(a-1)\\,x + (a-1) .\n\\]\n\nEquivalently, \\(\\ell_a\\) is the unique line of slope \\(1\\) passing through the\ntwo extreme points of the column \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}\\), namely\n\\((a,1)\\) and \\((a,n+1-a)\\).  Because its slope is \\(1\\), \\(\\ell_a\\) is not\nparallel to any of the three forbidden directions; hence each \\(\\ell_a\\) is\nsunny.  Distinct values of \\(a\\) give distinct lines, so we obtain exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a\\le t\\) lies on the vertical line\n\\(x=a\\) from (9).  \nIf \\(a>t\\) then \\(a\\) belongs to the set (10); the line \\(\\ell_a\\) contains\nall points of that column, in particular \\((a,b)\\).  Thus every point of\n\\(S_n\\) is covered.\n\nThe total number of lines is \\(t+k=n\\), and the number of sunny lines is\nexactly \\(k\\).  Hence for each admissible \\(k\\) we have produced a suitable\nfamily of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof\n\nInequality (8) shows that no configuration can have more than\n\\(\\lfloor n/3\\rfloor\\) sunny lines.  \nThe construction of \u00a72 shows that every integer\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/3\\rfloor\\) is attainable.  \n\nTherefore the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/3\\rfloor\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution."
