            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called *sunny* iff it is not parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\nx=\\text{const},\\qquad y=\\text{const},\\qquad x+y=\\text{const}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nConsequently a non‑sunny line belongs to exactly one of the three families\nin (1).  Denote  \n\n\\[\nA=\\{a\\mid x=a\\text{ is used}\\},\\;\nB=\\{b\\mid y=b\\text{ is used}\\},\\;\nC=\\{c\\mid x+y=c\\text{ is used}\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe number of non‑sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\nt=|A|+|B|+|C| . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) is covered iff  \n\n\\[\na\\in A\\;\\text{or}\\;b\\in B\\;\\text{or}\\;a+b\\in C . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A lower bound for the number of non‑sunny lines\n\nDefine the complementary sets  \n\n\\[\nX=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus A,\\qquad\nY=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus B,\\qquad\nZ=\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus C .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nTheir cardinalities satisfy  \n\n\\[\n|X|+|Y|+|Z|=3n-(|A|+|B|+|C|)=3n-t . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nIf a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) is **not** covered, then\n\\(a\\in X,\\;b\\in Y,\\;a+b\\in Z\\).  Hence the existence of an uncovered point\nis equivalent to the existence of a triple \\((a,b,a+b)\\) with the three\ncomponents taken respectively from \\(X,Y,Z\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma  \n\nLet \\(X\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\;Y\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\;Z\\subseteq\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).\nIf  \n\n\\[\n|X|+|Y|+|Z|>n+1,\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nthen there exist \\(a\\in X,\\;b\\in Y\\) such that \\(a+b\\in Z\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the set of all possible sums  \n\n\\[\n\\Sigma=\\{a+b\\mid a\\in X,\\;b\\in Y\\}\\subseteq\\{2,\\dots ,2n\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) let \\(m(s)\\) be the number of representations of \\(s\\) as\n\\(a+b\\) with \\(a\\in X,\\;b\\in Y\\).  Obviously  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{s=2}^{2n} m(s)=|X|\\cdot|Y| . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe interval \\([2,2n]\\) contains \\(2n-1\\) integers.  Hence by the pigeonhole\nprinciple there exists a sum \\(s_0\\) with  \n\n\\[\nm(s_0)\\ge\\frac{|X|\\cdot|Y|}{2n-1}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(s_0\\in Z\\) we are done.  Suppose \\(s_0\\notin Z\\).  Then every integer of\n\\(Z\\) is different from \\(s_0\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n|Z|\\le (2n-1)-1 =2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nUsing (7) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n|X|+|Y| > n+1-|Z|\\ge n+1-(2n-2)=3-n .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n\\ge3\\), this forces \\(|X|+|Y|\\ge n\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n|X|\\cdot|Y|\\ge n\\cdot1\\ge n .\n\\]\n\nFrom (9) we get \\(m(s_0)\\ge 1\\), i.e. there is at least one representation\n\\(s_0=a+b\\) with \\(a\\in X,\\;b\\in Y\\).  Because \\(s_0\\notin Z\\) we would have an\nuncovered point, contradicting the assumption that all points of \\(S_n\\) are\ncovered.  Therefore the supposition \\(s_0\\notin Z\\) is impossible, and a sum\n\\(a+b\\in Z\\) must exist. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nApplying the lemma to the sets (5) and using (6) we obtain\n\n\\[\n3n-t>n+1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;t\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{2n}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThus any family of \\(n\\) lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) must contain at\nleast \\(\\lceil2n/3\\rceil\\) non‑sunny lines.  Consequently the number \\(k\\) of\nsunny lines satisfies\n\n\\[\nk=n-t\\le n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{2n}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nm=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\\qquad\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,m\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  A fixed family of non‑sunny lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nA_0=\\{a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\mid a\\equiv0\\pmod3\\},\n\\qquad\nB_0=\\{b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\mid b\\equiv1\\pmod3\\},\n\\]\n\\[\nC_0=\\{c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\mid c\\equiv2\\pmod3\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsider the three collections of lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_A=\\{x=a\\mid a\\in A_0\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal L_B=\\{y=b\\mid b\\in B_0\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal L_C=\\{x+y=c\\mid c\\in C_0\\}.\n\\]\n\nTheir cardinalities are  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L_A|=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\\qquad\n|\\mathcal L_B|=|\\mathcal L_C|=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nHence the total number of non‑sunny lines in  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N:=\\mathcal L_A\\cup\\mathcal L_B\\cup\\mathcal L_C\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nis  \n\n\\[\nt_0=|\\mathcal N|\n    =\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{2n}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\n*Why \\(\\mathcal N\\) covers \\(S_n\\).*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) the residues of the three integers\n\\(a,\\;b,\\;a+b\\) modulo \\(3\\) form a permutation of \\(\\{0,1,2\\}\\).  Therefore at\nleast one of the following holds:\n\n* \\(a\\equiv0\\pmod3\\) – then the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal L_A\\) passes\n  through \\((a,b)\\);\n* \\(b\\equiv1\\pmod3\\) – then the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal L_B\\) passes\n  through \\((a,b)\\);\n* \\(a+b\\equiv2\\pmod3\\) – then the diagonal line \\(x+y=a+b\\in\\mathcal L_C\\) passes\n  through \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal N\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Adding sunny lines\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal N\\) contains \\(t_0\\) lines, while we need exactly \\(n\\)\nlines.  The deficit is  \n\n\\[\nn-t_0=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor =m .\n\\]\n\nChoose any \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines (for instance, lines of slope \\(2\\) that\npass through already covered points) and add them to \\(\\mathcal N\\).  The\nresulting set\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F:=\\mathcal N\\;\\cup\\;\\{\\text{the chosen }k\\text{ sunny lines}\\}\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\ncontains  \n\n\\[\nt_0\\;(\\text{non‑sunny})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;=\\;n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines and still covers all points of \\(S_n\\).  The number of sunny\nlines in \\(\\mathcal F\\) is exactly the prescribed \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Obtaining smaller values of \\(k\\)\n\nIf a smaller number of sunny lines is required, replace some of the added\nsunny lines by extra non‑sunny lines whose defining constants lie outside the\nranges \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (e.g. \\(x=n+1\\), \\(y=n+2\\), \\(x+y=n+3\\)).  Such lines\ndo not intersect any point of \\(S_n\\) and therefore do not affect the covering,\nbut they increase the count of non‑sunny lines and consequently decrease the\nnumber of sunny lines.  By performing this replacement repeatedly we can\nrealise any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le m\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof\n\nInequality (11) shows that no configuration of \\(n\\) lines can have more than\n\\(\\lfloor n/3\\rfloor\\) sunny lines.  The construction described in Sections 2.1–2.3\nproduces, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/3\\rfloor\\), a family\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.  Hence the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor n/3\\bigr\\rfloor\\,\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThis completes the solution.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
