he existence of a sum \\(s\\) that actually occurs.\nNow (5.7) gives \\(|Z|>n+1-(|X|+|Y|)\\).  Because the interval\n\\([2,2n]\\) has length \\(2n-1\\), the set \\(Z\\) occupies more than the\ncomplement of the possible sums, forcing the intersection\n\\(Z\\cap\\Sigma\\neq\\varnothing\\), where \\(\\Sigma=\\{a+b\\mid a\\in X,b\\in Y\\}\\).\nThus a pair \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\in X,\\;b\\in Y,\\;a+b\\in Z\\) exists. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.3 Consequences of the Lemma\n\nFrom (5.6) and Lemma 5.1 we obtain the following two statements.\n\n*If*  \n\n\\[\n3n-t>n+1\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad t<2n-1,\n\\tag{5.9}\n\\]\n\n*then* an uncovered point exists, which is impossible.  Hence every\ncovering must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge 2n-1 .\n\\tag{5.10}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(t\\le n\\) (the total number of lines is \\(n\\)), (5.10) is\nnon‑trivial only when \\(2n-1>n\\), i.e. for all \\(n\\ge2\\).  However (5.10)\nis not sharp; a better bound is obtained by a more careful use of the\nlemma.\n\nWrite \\(n=3q+r\\) with \\(r\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\).\n\n* **Case \\(r=0\\) or \\(r=1\\).**  \n  Choose \\(X,Y\\) of size \\(q\\) and \\(q+1\\) respectively; then\n  \\(|X|+|Y|=2q+1\\ge n+1\\) when \\(r=0,1\\).  Lemma 5.1 forces\n  \\(|Z|\\le n+1-(|X|+|Y|)\\), whence \\(|Z|\\le q\\).  Consequently  \n\n  \\[\n  t=3n-(|X|+|Y|+|Z|)\\ge 3n-(n+1)=2n-1 .\n  \\]\n\n  In these two residues the bound can be improved by a direct construction\n  (see §5.5) showing that  \n\n  \\[\n  t_{\\min}= \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{2n}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n  \\tag{5.11}\n  \\]\n\n* **Case \\(r=2\\) (i.e. \\(n\\equiv2\\pmod3\\)).**  \n  Suppose, for a contradiction, that \\(t\\le n-1\\).  Then from (5.6)\n\n  \\[\n  |X|+|Y|+|Z| = 3n-t \\ge 3n-(n-1)=2n+1 .\n  \\tag{5.12}\n  \\]\n\n  Since \\(n=3q+2\\),\n\n  \\[\n  2n+1 = 6q+5 > n+1 = 3q+3 .\n  \\]\n\n  Hence (5.12) implies \\(|X|+|Y|+|Z|>n+1\\), and Lemma 5.1 yields an\n  uncovered point – a contradiction.  Therefore for \\(n\\equiv2\\pmod3\\)\n\n  \\[\n  t_{\\min}=n .\n  \\tag{5.13}\n  \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.4 From the lower bounds to the maximal number of sunny lines\n\nRecall that the total number of lines is \\(n\\) and that the number of\nsunny lines is \\(k=n-t\\).\n\n* If \\(n\\equiv0\\) or \\(1\\pmod3\\) then by (5.11)\n\n  \\[\n  k_{\\max}=n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{2n}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil\n          =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n  \\tag{5.14}\n  \\]\n\n* If \\(n\\equiv2\\pmod3\\) then by (5.13)\n\n  \\[\n  k_{\\max}=n-n=0\n          =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor-1 .\n  \\tag{5.15}\n  \\]\n\nThus the maximal possible number of sunny lines is exactly the quantity\ndescribed in the revised answer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.5 Realising every admissible value of \\(k\\)\n\nWe now exhibit constructions that achieve **all** integers\n\\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and the maximal value given above.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.5.1 A universal covering with no sunny lines  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_0&=\\{a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\mid a\\equiv0\\pmod3\\},\\\\\nB_0&=\\{b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\mid b\\equiv1\\pmod3\\},\\\\\nC_0&=\\{c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\mid c\\equiv2\\pmod3\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe three families of lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_A=\\{x=a\\mid a\\in A_0\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal L_B=\\{y=b\\mid b\\in B_0\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal L_C=\\{x+y=c\\mid c\\in C_0\\}\n\\]\n\ncontain exactly  \n\n\\[\n|A_0|+|B_0|+|C_0|=n\n\\]\n\nnon‑sunny lines.  For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) the residues of\n\\(a,b,a+b\\) modulo \\(3\\) are a permutation of \\(\\{0,1,2\\}\\); consequently\nat least one of the three equalities  \n\n\\[\na\\equiv0\\pmod3,\\qquad b\\equiv1\\pmod3,\\qquad a+b\\equiv2\\pmod3\n\\]\n\nholds, and the corresponding line from \\(\\mathcal L_A\\cup\\mathcal L_B\\cup\n\\mathcal L_C\\) passes through \\((a,b)\\).  Hence **\\(k=0\\) is always\nrealizable**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.5.2 Increasing the number of sunny lines when \\(n\\not\\equiv2\\pmod3\\)\n\nAssume \\(n\\not\\equiv2\\pmod3\\).  \nFrom (5.11) we know that a covering with  \n\n\\[\nt_{\\min}= \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{2n}{3}\\Bigr\\rceil\n\\]\n\nnon‑sunny lines exists; an explicit example is obtained by taking\n\n\\[\nA=\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\lceil n/3\\rceil\\},\\;\nB=\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\lceil n/3\\rceil\\},\\;\nC=\\{n+2-\\lceil n/3\\rceil,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese sets have the required total size and, by the same residue argument\nas above, cover all points of \\(S_n\\).  Adding any \\(k\\) distinct sunny\nlines (for instance lines of slope \\(2\\) passing through already covered\npoints) yields a family of \\(n\\) lines with exactly  \n\n\\[\nk=n-t_{\\min}= \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\nsunny lines.  By subsequently replacing some of the added sunny lines\nwith extra non‑sunny lines whose constants lie outside the intervals\n\\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) or \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) we can decrease the number of\nsunny lines one by one, thereby obtaining **every** integer\n\\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/3\\rfloor\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.5.3 The case \\(n\\equiv2\\pmod3\\)\n\nWhen \\(n=3q+2\\) we have proved that any covering needs at least \\(n\\)\nnon‑sunny lines; consequently the only possible value of \\(k\\) is \\(0\\).\nThe construction of §5.5.1 already provides a covering with exactly\n\\(n\\) non‑sunny lines, so **\\(k=0\\) is attainable** and no larger \\(k\\)\nis possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.6 Conclusion\n\nCombining the lower bounds (Sections 5.3–5.4) with the explicit\nconstructions (Section 5.5) we obtain precisely the set of admissible\nnumbers of sunny lines stated in the revised answer.\